  "summary_text": "Kyle, Labor Day closed Port Washington yesterday entirely\u2014zero revenue, zero appointments\u2014which is expected structural impact, not operational failure. Hicksville similarly showed zero activity. The real signal sits in September month-to-date: Port Washington is running 22.6% below DOW-adjusted expectation ($16,790 actual vs $21,690 expected), and Hicksville is down 11.8% ($7,215 actual vs $8,183 expected). This is real performance leakage, not calendar mix. Both stores are showing the same pattern: appointment count is collapsing harder than revenue (Port down 34% appointments, Hicksville flat on appointments but down 7% revenue), which suggests a capacity problem masking as a demand problem.\n\nHere's what demands immediate attention: Port Washington groomer-days are down 26% month-to-date (25 vs 34 last month) and bather hours have evaporated (0 yesterday, trailing 30-day average 5.9h/day). This staffing cliff is the root cause of the miss. Hicksville's productivity per groomer is also declining\u2014$388/groomer-day versus $412 last month\u2014signaling either scheduling inefficiency or groomer utilization gaps. Both stores have meaningful lapse pools (Port: 505 customers worth $318k annually; Hicksville: 160 customers worth $119k). These are reactivation opportunities, but you can't pursue them without capacity to fulfill.\n\nWhat you should be proud of: year-to-date Port Washington is up 43% in revenue and 42% in appointments versus 2025. You've built real momentum.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Today you have 10 groomer-days scheduled across both stores (7 Port, 3 Hicksville) with zero bather coverage; tomorrow adds 10 groomer-days with 15 bather hours at Port only; next 7 days you're scheduled for 58 groomer-days and 83 bather hours, which is insufficient to close the MTD gap.**"
